#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119530 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "0e45ac96-0eee-51a3-83e0-192582dcf57d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4765" :question/problem "Given $\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ -7 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$ find its LU decomposition." :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U$, we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ -7 & -6\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + 1 \\cdot R_{1}⟺\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(E_{1}) \\cdot A \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= U\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U$. Now, since $E_1A=U$, we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{1}A &= U \\\\\nA &= E_{1}^{-1}U \\\\\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}U \\\\\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(L)\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(U)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119531 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6093e24d-14bb-5e3b-a0b4-7a562a12f6a2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a Singular 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "e1d1f80c-70d1-566d-9cc0-94899b0fa05d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4767" :question/problem "Given\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{bmatrix}$, find its LU decomposition." :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U$, we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + (-3)R_{1}⟺\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(E_{1}) \\cdot A \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= U\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U$. Now, since $E_1A=U$, we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{1}A &= U \\\\\nA &= E_{1}^{-1}U \\\\\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}U \\\\\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(L)\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(U)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119592 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying LU Factorizations"}] :question/id #uuid "ea39d5ec-9c8f-572d-8708-fdd97d0c04bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-6184" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A$?\n\n1. $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n2. $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\n3. $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A$, we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although the left matrix is a lower triangular matrix, it is not a *unit* lower triangular matrix because it does not have $1$'s on the diagonal.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99f1aa48-56e9-50bd-913a-a86035020cd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117229} {:answer/id #uuid "420525a8-83ac-5d3c-a47a-051b310d6d56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -16 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117230} {:answer/id #uuid "5e36d3c8-6349-5e0d-9791-13e937e9836b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117231} {:answer/id #uuid "6a82d757-cd2a-55c4-a3c6-1b911242d528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117232} {:answer/id #uuid "7a6aff11-5c60-5ce9-8ddb-3fc56281a507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99f1aa48-56e9-50bd-913a-a86035020cd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117229} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c61e6d7-1ff8-54d2-b73c-05b52de377ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117228}] :question/id #uuid "363a3b99-7f36-55a2-ab26-35e7a3af8462" :question/math-academy-id "q-32549" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -16 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nhas the LU decomposition $A = LU$, then $L =$"}]}
